# Runtime state, not source. Celery beat writes its "last fired" database on
# every start, so a committed copy hands everyone else this machine's schedule
# history — and beat may then skip a run it believes already happened. The
# suffix varies by platform because it is a shelve/dbm file.
celerybeat-schedule*
celerybeat.pid
dump.rdb
